hi I'm Evan Carmichael welcome to  another edition of modeling the masters  today's video is brought to you by MX  for business camera they launched a new  Facebook page that has a lot of  resources information ideas for  entrepreneurs that I hope you go check  out if you like the videos please go  check out the Facebook page now I  believe that the fastest and most  effective way to grow your business is  to model the strategies of people who  have already done what you're trying to  do so today we're gonna look at how a  Canadian entrepreneur who was arrested  for bootlegging t-shirts bringing them  across the border went from that to  becoming the largest t-shirt  manufacturer in North America this is a  story of Canadian entrepreneur Dov  Charney and the top three lessons you  can learn from his success he is the  founder of American Apparel Dov Charney  was born in Montreal Canada to Jewish  parents but was sent to Wallingford  Connecticut to attend a prestigious prep  school to control his behavior  Charney was a self-described hyperactive  child and was frustrating his parents  with his behavior who would focus on  just about everything else but the  school he was attending while ignoring  schoolwork he would find a money-making  opportunity by purchasing t-shirts at a  Kmart and taking them across the border  of Canada where he would sell them for a  profit journey would then close a large  order for t-shirts but the order went  south and he would eventually lose more  than $100,000 this business disaster  would make charlie quit the t-shirt  business for a while and go to college  he found that the t-shirt business was  calling him again so he dropped out of  Tufts University in his senior year to  follow his passion after moodling the  South Carolina and getting a ten  thousand dollar loan from his father  journey would start a new t-shirt  business in 1989 American Apparel today  American Apparel has over five hundred  million dollars in revenue and over  eleven thousand employees  it's the largest clothing manufacturer  in the United States and has almost 300  locations worldwide  in 2004 China was named Ernst &amp; Young's  entrepreneur of the year and he's made  quite a return for himself on that  initial ten thousand dollar loan  by following his passion and by not  giving up after a big defeat Charney was  able to go from small-time bootlegger  into the largest t-shirt manufacturer in  North America to help you amp up your  business here are three action items and  you can learn from Dov Charney action  item number one narrow the market  you can't sell to everyone possessive  entrepreneurs hone their offering around  a specific target market and ignore the  rest of the world when you design for  everyone nobody ends up loving you they  might love pieces of what you do but  it's never perfect for them when you  design for a specific market you can  make your product or service amazing for  them solve their problems and forget  about everyone else journey knows who's  buying his clothes and it's a very  specific market he's tailoring his  designs towards twenty-somethings which  means tighter and sucks your clothes in  the baby boomer generation before them  were used to it also means given as  clothes bright and bold colors journey  identifies with this market a group of  people who are more socially minded than  their predecessors to the end he decided  to market his company as not only brand  free but also locally made and sweatshop  free as he sees it journey has connected  with an emerging youth movement a  network of new hip youths that want cool  clothes but do not want to be walking  advertisements for giant corporations by  narrowing his market he has created the  largest t-shirt manufacturer in the  country according the Charney  there's the relaxed fit generation and  then there's the next generation we like  sexy at American Apparel we don't have  branding on the shirts it's not a status  symbol  I can't wear any brand on my body I just  freak out  action item number to innovate going  exploit success depends on innovation if  you want to stand out and be different  from your competitors you need to find  innovative ways to separate yourself  innovation takes many forms  it could be with your products or  services business practices company  culture customer service and the list  goes on  if you don't innovate you'll risk being  an average company that most people  ignore and forget about it  American Apparel is about much more than  clothes or at least Charney hope so in  creating the company of his dreams  journey wanted to make sure that it  stood for something that it was selling  a message along with its products that  message is that a company does not have  to be exploited if to turn a profit he  make sure his employees not only get a  decent base wage but can make more by  working harder Charney also implements  several new t-shirts every month to keep  his customers looking for new and  creative t-shirts to wear not only are  his employees happy but his customers  have several innovative choices when  buying one of his t-shirts  according to Charney we designed the  rate in such a way that the average  person should be able to make $100 a day  that's our target we want to pay more  than the prevailing wages in Los Angeles  because we want to have the happiest  workforce we can have what I'm gonna  prove and I'm gonna embarrass the entire  establishment is that sweatshops are  more expensive in the end than  vertically integrated manufacturing in  Canada or the US you see those prisons  in China are inefficient and the  opportunity cost of offshore production  is huge because you can't respond to  market demands as quickly  action item number three deficiency is  the key as entrepreneurs we tend to be  time starved  there's always much more that needs to  be done and not enough time of the day  to do it focus on what you do best and  find ways to make everything else more  efficient  it starts with eliminating tasks that  are not really adding a lot of value to  your business then automate as much as  possible through technology finally you  can delegate out the rest of the tasks  that you don't personally bring as much  value - leaving only the high value high  enjoyment tasks for you to work on  you'll grow your business and have much  more fun in the process before building  American Apparel to what it is today  Charney had to file for bankruptcy it  was because his company was suffering  from a lack of one thing efficiency the  staff for all in her 20s a wildly  creative Bunch when it came to graphics  and design but few knew the ins and outs  of running the business in response  Charney brought on marty bailey the  industry expert will help make  efficiency American Apparel secret  weapon against his competitors one of  his main strategies was to create what  he calls team manufacturing where groups  of eight to ten people work on a single  garment together each performing a  different task being more efficient  during the manufacturing process  allows American Apparel to grow and  Charney's determined to remain as  efficient as possible according to  charming as a result of this system were  able to compete with China and kick ass  the American way it's less expensive for  me the way we do business to manufacture  here in the United States there's a high  cost of going offshore if you're working  with a supplier in China you've got to  work months in advance if you're working  with your own factory you can wake up  one morning and say hey let's make ten  thousand tank tops today  so remember narrow your market innovate  and be efficient to finish up this video  I wanted to share what on my favorite  true stories about Dov Charney and some  of his best quotes love Charney was such  a hyperactive child that his Canadian  parents sent him to a prep school in  Connecticut there you would see many of  his classmates selling t-shirts on  school grounds  this gave Charney an idea of buying  t-shirts from the local Kmart and taken  them across the border into Canada to  sell for profit which happened to be  against the law during one of his  bootlegging excursions he was arrested  they took me down a station ten which  doesn't exist anymore and after a couple  of hours of me yelling  masoom Monsieur they let me out and gave  me back my cash and my shirts recalls  Charney so what did i do headed straight  for the [ __ ] and bull to try and unload  the rest of them even getting arrested  did not deter attorney from selling his  t-shirts  thank you for joining me for another  dish of the model in the masters if you  liked the video and you want to see more  please give it a thumbs up below I'd  also love to hear what you have to think  if you want to leave a comment under the  video and please make sure you go check  out the mx2 business kind of a Facebook  page they supported us in making these  videos so thank you and stay tuned if  next episode  Oh
 

